# After `@: size_t p1 @v` the working p1 holds @v and is dirty; its published cell holds what it held, and p2 = @p1
# is that cell's address (L2 section 18.2; semantics section 12: address acquisition is not a publication boundary).
# An ordinary user call after the declarations publishes p1: p2's pointee is then @v, and `\\p2: 5U` changes v's
# cell -- read through p2 and p1 -- while the bare working v keeps 0. Without the call the first load of the double
# dereference reads the unpublished cell (a publication-state observation, not a test outcome). Codex 2026-10-06,
# control A. Success is 7.
fn: publish () int
    return: 0
end: publish
fn: check () int
    size_t: v 0U
    @: size_t p1 @v
    @@: size_t p2 @p1
    int: done publish()
    if: \p2 != @v
        return: 1
    \\p2: 5U
    if: \\p2 != 5U
        return: 2
    if: \p1 != 5U
        return: 3
    if: v != 0U
        return: 4
    return: 7
end: check
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: check(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
